## Confusion matrix (validation set)

Heatmap showing the proportion of cells with each true label (rows) that are predicted as each class (columns). A perfect classifier would show signal only on the diagonal.

```{r confusion_matrix, fig.height = 10, fig.width = 12}
# prepare inputs for plot_cluster_comparison_heatmap
cl1_dt = val_dt[, .(cell_id, true_label = label %>% factor)]
cl2_dt = val_dt[, .(cell_id, pred_label = predicted_label %>% factor)]

confuse_dt = calc_confuse_dt(cl1_dt, cl2_dt, "true_label", "pred_label")
plot_cluster_comparison_heatmap(confuse_dt, "true_label", "pred_label",
  plot_var = "p_cl1", do_sort = "hclust")
```


## Per-class evaluation metrics (validation set)

**Accuracy**: fraction of cells with this true label that are correctly predicted.
**Precision**: of all cells predicted as this class, fraction that truly are.
**Recall**: same as accuracy (fraction of true positives captured).
**F1**: harmonic mean of precision and recall.

```{r metrics_table}
metrics_dt = val_dt[, .(
  N         = .N,
  Accuracy  = mean(label == predicted_label),
  Precision = {
    tp = sum(predicted_label == label)
    fp = nrow(val_dt[ predicted_label == .BY$label & label != .BY$label ])
    tp / max(tp + fp, 1)
  },
  Recall    = mean(label == predicted_label),
  F1        = {
    prec = sum(predicted_label == label) / max(sum(val_dt$predicted_label == .BY$label), 1)
    rec  = mean(label == predicted_label)
    2 * prec * rec / max(prec + rec, 1e-10)
  }
), by = .(Class = label)] %>% .[ order(-Accuracy) ]

metrics_dt %>%
  kable(digits = 3) %>%
  kable_styling(bootstrap_options = c("striped", "hover", "condensed"), full_width = FALSE)
```

**Overall accuracy**: `r round(val_dt[, mean(label == predicted_label)], 3)`
**Macro F1**: `r round(mean(metrics_dt$F1), 3)`


## Per-class prediction confidence (validation set)

Distribution of the maximum predicted probability for each cell, grouped by true cell type. Cell types where the model is uncertain will show lower probabilities. Very low confidence may indicate that a class is underrepresented or too similar to another class.

```{r confidence_violin, fig.height = 7, fig.width = 10}
ggplot(val_dt) +
  aes(x = fct_reorder(label, probability, .fun = median), y = probability, fill = label) +
  geom_violin(scale = "width", show.legend = FALSE) +
  geom_boxplot(width = 0.1, outlier.size = 0.3, show.legend = FALSE) +
  coord_flip() +
  theme_bw() +
  labs(x = "True label", y = "Max predicted probability")
```


## Feature selection

### Cumulative gain curve

Genes are ranked by their contribution to XGBoost's loss reduction (gain). The vertical line shows the cutoff where the top genes capture 90% of total gain. Genes to the left of the line are included in the final model.

```{r gain_curve, fig.height = 5, fig.width = 8}
ggplot(imp_dt) +
  aes(x = seq_len(.N), y = cumulative_gain_frac) +
  geom_line() +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.9, linetype = "dashed", colour = "red") +
  geom_vline(xintercept = sum(imp_dt$cumulative_gain_frac <= 0.9) + 1,
             linetype = "dashed", colour = "blue") +
  theme_bw() +
  labs(x = "Gene rank", y = "Cumulative gain fraction",
       title = "Feature selection: cumulative gain curve") +
  annotate("text", x = sum(imp_dt$cumulative_gain_frac <= 0.9) + 1,
           y = 0.5, label = paste0(sum(imp_dt$cumulative_gain_frac <= 0.9) + 1, " genes"),
           hjust = -0.1, colour = "blue")
```


### Top 30 genes by importance

```{r gene_importance, fig.height = 7, fig.width = 8}
top_genes = imp_dt[1:min(30, .N)]
ggplot(top_genes) +
  aes(x = fct_reorder(gene, gain), y = gain) +
  geom_col(fill = "steelblue") +
  coord_flip() +
  theme_bw() +
  labs(x = "Gene", y = "Gain", title = "Top 30 genes by XGBoost gain")
```
